<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#tin">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tin is a metal that, when alloyed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, produces bronze, making it a strategic commodity of the ancient world. Because tin deposits were rare, it was traded over long distances. Assyrian merchants at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanesh">Kanesh</a> imported tin into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> participated in the tin and copper trade, and Phoenician and Carthaginian voyages sought western sources of tin, as described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a> profile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tin.jpg" alt="Tin" width="320" height="147" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ondřej Mangl · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C%C3%ADn.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
